# =============================================================================
# labwc autostart — what runs once the window manager is up
# =============================================================================
# labwc runs this file as a shell script after it has read rc.xml and the
# `environment` file next to this one. By that point WAYLAND_DISPLAY exists,
# which is why the shell can only be started from here and not from
# /usr/bin/aquarius-session.
#
# NO MACHINE-SPECIFIC PATHS IN THIS FILE. The shell is started with a bare `qs`,
# which finds its configuration through QS_CONFIG_PATH, exported by
# /usr/bin/aquarius-session before labwc was launched. Same for $AQ_LOG.
#
# (This file is the AquariusOS copy of the one in the aquarius-shell repository.
# The niri equivalent of every line here is in that repository's
# session/niri/config.kdl, which AquariusOS does not ship.)
# =============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Tell systemd and D-Bus that a screen now exists.
#
# The launcher already pushed XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP and friends across, but
# WAYLAND_DISPLAY did not exist yet — labwc had not started. Anything launched
# on demand from now on (the portals, the keyring, a Flatpak's file dialog)
# needs it, and a portal that does not know which Wayland display to talk to
# fails silently rather than loudly.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if command -v dbus-update-activation-environment > /dev/null 2>&1; then
    dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd WAYLAND_DISPLAY DISPLAY 2>/dev/null || true
else
    systemctl --user import-environment WAYLAND_DISPLAY DISPLAY 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Tell systemd the graphical session has begun.
#
# Portals, notification daemons and keyring agents are systemd user services
# that say "start me with graphical-session.target". labwc ships
# labwc-session.target, which binds to that target, but labwc does not start it
# itself — starting it is opt-in, and this line is the opt-in.
#
# Without it the portals may never start, and screen recording and file dialogs
# quietly do nothing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
systemctl --user --no-block start labwc-session.target 2>/dev/null || true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. The wallpaper.
#
# A window manager on its own paints the screen a flat grey. swaybg puts the
# Aquarius wallpaper behind everything, which matters more than it sounds: the
# difference between "my computer is broken" and "my computer is starting" is
# often just whether the screen looks deliberate.
#
# This is here rather than in the shell because a wallpaper should survive the
# shell crashing. If the bar dies you should still be looking at your desktop.
#
# --mode fill crops to the screen rather than stretching; -c is the colour
# behind the image while it loads, and it is the Midnight void from the boot
# splash so the hand-off from Plymouth has no flash of grey.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
if [ -r /usr/share/backgrounds/aquarius/the-pour-ice-3840x2160.png ]; then
    swaybg -c '#06070C' \
        -i /usr/share/backgrounds/aquarius/the-pour-ice-3840x2160.png \
        -m fill >> "${AQ_LOG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 &
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. The Aquarius Shell.
#
# Started through a helper rather than directly, for one reason: if the shell
# fails, somebody has to say so on the screen. The helper runs it, logs it, and
# if it dies puts a dialog in front of you explaining what happened and how to
# get out. /usr/libexec/aquarius-shell-start is worth reading — it is short.
#
# If the shell crashes, labwc keeps running and you keep your windows. You get a
# desktop with no bar, which is ugly but survivable, and typing `qs` in a
# terminal brings it back. That is deliberately better than the alternative,
# where a broken bar logs you out — and it is why aquarius-session starts labwc
# without its -S/--session flag, which would tie the window manager's life to
# the shell's.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/usr/libexec/aquarius-shell-start &
